+ The `JavaRuntime` entry can say what it is made of, which a tool that indexes or documents the
+ JDK's own API needs. `modules` names the runtime's modules, `exports` the packages which a
+ module exports to every other module, and `classes` the classes of one of those packages, each
+ by its full name:
+
+ ```scala fuel
+ run
+ ##
+ val runtime = Classpath.Entry.JavaRuntime
+
+ Out.println(runtime.modules.has(t"java.base").inspect)
+ Out.println(runtime.exports(t"java.base").has(t"java.util").inspect)
+ Out.println(runtime.classes(t"java.base", t"java.util").has(t"java.util.Optional").inspect)
+ ```
+
+ A package which a module exports only to named modules is not among its `exports`, and a
+ nested class is named as the runtime names it, such as `java.util.Map$Entry`. A module or a
+ package which does not exist has nothing in it, so each gives an empty list. These are for
+ the JVM only: a program compiled by Scala Native has no runtime image to list.
+
